fact WorkItem(id: "WI-919", description: some(value: "A RESOLVED SYMBOL IS RENDERED BACK TO A NAME TO ASK A POLICY QUESTION, AND THE MISS IS SILENT — the closest remaining twin of WI-916's shape, one seam over. `Interpreter::resolve_fact_monotonicity` (rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs ~4183) takes a `Symbol`, and when no reflect rule answers it does `let qname = self.kb.qualified_name_of(functor).to_string(); self.kb.mirror_monotonicity(&qname).unwrap_or(Monotonicity::Monotone)` — a Symbol turned back into a String to key `ExtentRegistry.mirror_monotonicity: HashMap<String, Monotonicity>` (kb/extent.rs ~406), which a mirror populated from `Store::owned_monotonicity() -> Vec<(String, Monotonicity)>` at registration. TWO THINGS. (1) The round trip: a resolved identity is re-derived as text to look up a table the registration could have keyed on the Symbol it was about to resolve anyway — the WI-916 defect with the arrow reversed (there a name was re-resolved; here an identity is re-named). The field carries a STATED reason — \"Names remain strings because a backend may name a functor that is only interned after bootstrap\" — so this is a decision to re-examine, not an assumed bug: check whether that ordering is still real now that `register_extent_owner` resolves owner names at registration, and if it is, whether the mirror can resolve lazily-but-once rather than per call. (2) The silent default is worse than the round trip: `.unwrap_or(Monotonicity::Monotone)` means a key MISS is indistinguishable from a mirror that declared Monotone, and `Monotone` is what the proposal-053 guard refuses to retract — so a name that does not match its table's spelling surfaces to the author as \"retract refused: functor `X` is not non_monotone (proposal 053)\", a policy verdict, when nothing declared any policy at all. That is the repo's silent-skip rule broken in the direction that looks like a decision. NOT DRIVEN — code-read, raised while delivering WI-916. FIRST STEP: drive the miss — register a mirror whose `owned_monotonicity` names a functor by one spelling and retract through a reference whose `qualified_name_of` gives another, and assert what the author is told. ACCEPTANCE: a mirror policy lookup that misses is distinguishable from one that answered Monotone (either a loud refusal naming the unmatched spelling, or the lookup is Symbol-keyed and the miss cannot arise); the `Monotone` default is a stated decision at its site either way; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-01T07:09:39Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-919", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (commit 89247a23, branch wi919-mirror-policy-keying). Full workspace 3959/0 green.\n\nDRIVEN FIRST, as the ticket's FIRST STEP asked, and both halves of its claim held. A PolicyStore declaring the SHORT name `Ghost` for a KB whose functor is `test.syn.Ghost`:\n\n  registration = accepted, silently\n  monotonicity = anthill.reflect.Monotonicity.monotone   <- rung 3, i.e. the miss\n  retract      = Err(Raised) `retract refused: functor test.syn.Ghost is not non_monotone (proposal 053)`\n\nThe control run (same store, qualified spelling) retracts Ok(true). So the author declared exactly that policy and is told their functor does not have it, in a message naming neither the store, nor the declaration, nor the spelling.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT, and it is NOT where the ticket's part (1) suggested. The ticket framed the round trip (Symbol rendered back to text) as one thing to fix and the silent default as another. They are one thing: the round trip is only harmful because the miss it can produce is unreportable at the READ. A miss at the read is legitimate and common — no store speaks for most functors, and that is precedence rung 3 — so no refusal can live there. The name question therefore moves to REGISTRATION, where \"the name you declared denotes nothing\" is both knowable and actionable: `register_mirror` resolves each declared name once and keys the table on `Symbol`. Rung 3's `monotone` now means only what rung 3 says.\n\nTHE `.unwrap_or(Monotone)` SURVIVES UNCHANGED, and that is the stated decision the acceptance asked for: it is the documented default, not a fallback for a lookup that failed. Its site now says so, and says why a miss can no longer mean anything else.\n\nTHE REFUSAL IS A REFUSAL, not a dropped entry, for the reason the driving exposed: the reader cannot tell a dropped entry from a functor no store spoke for, so a silent drop IS this ticket's defect, deferred to the next retract. The cost is stated at the site: declaring a policy for a functor this KB does not have is no longer expressible. Checked rather than assumed — no in-tree backend declares an intrinsic policy at all (the filesystem stores return []), and the field's old reason (\"a backend may name a functor that is only interned after bootstrap\") describes NO LIVE CALLER: the single production registration (anthill-todo/src/main.rs) runs after the project is loaded. If a schema-reading backend ever needs it, that is a decision to take deliberately.\n\nREVIEW'S WIN WAS DELETING MY OWN SECOND COPY. My first cut spelled the resolve-and-refuse match a SECOND time, verbatim beside `register_extent_owner`'s — the exact shape this ticket line keeps deleting, reintroduced inside the fix for it. Both registration paths now share one `registration_symbol`: a source's `owned()` functor and a mirror's `owned_monotonicity()` functor are the same question asked by the same kind of author, so the identical answer is now structural rather than asserted. The AMBIGUOUS arm came free with it, and is pinned by a test (a contested name at this site was also accepted in silence pre-fix, measured).\n\nAN UNVERIFIED VALUE OF MINE, caught before shipping: my first driver printed `monotonicity = Ok(Entity { functor: Symbol(2121) })` and I wrote 'monotone' into the test header from inference — the guard's message implies it — without checking. Re-measured through `Monotonicity::reflect_variant_qname`, which exists precisely to name a variant: it is `anthill.reflect.Monotonicity.monotone`. The header now carries the name, not my reading of a symbol index.\n\nA SILENT TOLERANCE I NEARLY LEFT: making `register_mirror` fallible turned 8 call sites into unused-`Result` WARNINGS rather than errors, which compile and run. Each is now handled — the anthill-todo caller reports and exits EXIT_RUNTIME, and each test `.expect`s with the reason its store cannot fail (the filesystem stores declare no policy).\n\nCONTROL: both refusals were measured accepted-in-silence against the pre-fix tree (the source files temporarily reverted, the driver adapted to the old `()` signature); the third test — the matching spelling registering AND its policy still reaching the guard — is green on both sides, as is all of wi667_store_monotonicity_test, every case of which registers a mirror.\n\nEFFICIENCY: better, not benchmarked. The guard path loses one String allocation per policy query (`qualified_name_of(functor).to_string()`) in favour of a `HashMap<Symbol, _>` lookup; it runs per mutation. Registration pays one ladder read per declared name, once.\n\n/code-review NOT RUN — user-invocable only; reminded. The four /simplify angles were applied inline (agents not spawned, per session instruction); that is not a substitute.\n\nNO NEW FOLLOW-UPS. WI-920 (the hardcoded `Fact` / `anthill.todo` sort+domain in the resident write seam), filed alongside this one, remains the open neighbour.", at: "2026-08-01T07:09:40Z")
